00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.960
John Kiriakou is a former CIA officer, former senior investigator for the Senate Foreign Relations

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:13.120
Committee, former counterterrorism consultant for ABC News. John was responsible for the capture

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:20.720
in Pakistan in 2002 at Abu Zubaydah, who was then believed to be the third-ranking official

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:27.760
in Al Qaeda. In 2007, Kiriakou blew the whistle on the CIA's torture program,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:36.240
telling ABC News that the CIA tortured prisoners, that torture was officially US government policy,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:43.920
that the policy had been approved by then-president George W. Bush. He became the sixth whistleblower

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:51.040
indicted by the Obama administration under the Espionage Act. A law designated to punish spies,

00:00:51.040 --> 00:01:00.080
he served 23 months in prison as a result of that revelation. In 2012, Kiriakou was honored with

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.480
the Joe A. Calloway Award for Civic Courage, an award given to individuals who, quote,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:13.840
advanced truth and justice despite the personal risk it creates, end quote, and by the inclusion

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.360
of his portrait in artist Robert Shetterley's series of Americans who tell the truth,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.400
which features notable truth tellers throughout American history. He won the Ben Center,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:33.600
